1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,800
At The Sea Ranch, {N} of {M} listed lots can't hold
a {X} square foot, {Y} foot house.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,000
No listing says so.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,800
The rules sit in a fifty-two page manual and county maps.
Buyers drive out to find the answer.

4
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:24,700
Lotline makes a lot's facts readable by an agent.
Every field is sourced. Every unknown is explicit.

5
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:34,700
Four tools over MCP, or a pay-per-call API.
One question: can I build here?

6
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:49,700
The buyer tells their agent what they want to build.

7
00:00:50,300 --> 00:01:04,700
The agent searches {n} lots. Each comes back with
what is known and what is not.

8
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:19,700
Verdicts are computed from the rules table, not by the model.
These lots fail on height, coverage, setbacks.
The agent never drives out to them.

9
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:29,700
A generic model guesses and sounds sure.
Here the answer is conditional, and every line has a source.

10
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:44,700
One lot passes everything we can check.
One rule is unknown: {FIELD}.
Unknown is an answer, not an error.

11
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:54,700
The unknown becomes a question for the seller,
drafted and sent by the agent.

12
00:01:55,300 --> 00:02:09,700
The seller answers once. The spec updates over
Supabase Realtime and the agent re-ranks the lots, live.

13
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:19,000
Every call is paid per use with Stripe's machine payments,
in sandbox.

14
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:24,700
Built on Supabase, Vercel, Stripe and Claude.

15
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,800
Lot facts first. Then design, financing and builders.
